When a decision is genuinely not yours — expensive to reverse, requirements that
contradict each other, access you do not have — do not guess and do not stall.
Record it:

    ilk ask-human open --decision "Should retries live in the gateway or the client?"

Write the question in `{{ index .Caps "ask.questions" }}/`, say what you would do absent an
answer, then carry on with everything the answer does not block. A blocking question
left open fails `ilk check`, so it cannot quietly become a decision nobody made.

Use `--decision` whenever you can name the two or three things it could be: each option
carries what picking it would mean, and you recommend one. That is the difference
between a question answerable in fifteen seconds and one that needs a working session.

Escalating is cheap; a wrong assumption compounding for three days is not.
